    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That sounds like a solid plan. I'm a bit worried about the actual communication flow on the night of the event though. Sometimes things get lost in translation between the front of house staff and the kitchen when it gets busy. \n\nHow can I ensure that the servers are actually paying attention to these specific labels and not just rushing past them? Is there a way to bake this check into their workflow so it feels seamless and not like an extra chore?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To prevent communication breakdowns, introduce a mandatory \"Alergy Check\" checkpoint at the entrance of the hospitality booths where runners must manually verify guest orders against a printed real-time list with a signature or digital confirmation. Train servers to treat allergen requests as highest-priority stops that absolutely cannot be skipped, perhaps by using color-coded order tags that physically catch their eye as they walk by the buffet. Additionally, consider adding a single, highly visible \"Allergy Alert\" poster in the kitchen holding a collaborative voice memo where the food director briefly reiterates these protocols right before service starts.",
      "source": "target",
